// Scalar integer tail loop: 100k iterations of (cmp, sub, add). The JIT
// lowers self-tail-calls to a native rebind-and-jump loop. The node-walk
// also handles tail self-calls iteratively (its GXLambda update loop), so
// it completes without overflowing the stack — this is the case where the
// interpreter does best relative to the JIT (a tight scalar loop with no
// per-element node-graph overhead), so the ratio here is the floor, not
// the headline. (Non-tail recursion like fib WOULD overflow the
// interpreter's native stack — that's a separate limitation.)
// CR claude for eric: [doc-drift] The node-walk has no tail loop
// (design/tail_calls_are_calls.md). It dispatches one retained activation per
// iteration, 100k here, and GXLambda::tail_loop gates only the JIT's native loop. Deep
// non-tail recursion does not overflow either: `1 + f(n - 1)` at depth 20000 returns
// under --no-fusion. leibniz_pi.gx:3-5,11-13 and bench/README.md:126-129 repeat the
// iterative node-walk claim; README:86-89 already says the opposite.
// leibniz_pi.gx:9-10's 'no unary minus on a variable' is also stale: `-sign` runs
// (ExprKind::Neg). (examples-08)
let rec sum_to = |n: i64, acc: i64| -> i64
  select n {
    0 => acc,
    _ => sum_to(n - 1, acc + n)
  };
let n = 100000;
let t0 = sys::time::now(0);
let seed = cast<i64>(t0)$;
let result = sum_to(n, seed);
let t1 = sys::time::now(result);
let elapsed = cast<f64>(t1)$ - cast<f64>(t0)$;
println("tail_sum n=[n] result=[result] elapsed_s=[elapsed]");
sys::exit(elapsed ~ 0)
